//
// A CALL INTO A SET GIVES NO TYPE HINT, AND THE REFUSAL NAMES THE CURE (`@FR-Disp-Hint`).  A literal
// that needs a width takes none from a set's call (write `5 as u8`), and an untyped lambda passed to
// a free set cannot infer its parameter.  Each function is one refused call with its expectation.
//
// ── a-literal-needing-a-width-at-a-set-is-refused ──
//
// A LITERAL NEEDING A WIDTH AT A SET IS REFUSED (`@FR-Disp-Hint`): the arguments of a name with
// several definitions are typed from their own spelling, so `hn(5)` is an `integer` call, which
// neither `hn(u8)` (no implicit narrowing) nor `hn(text)` takes — refused naming the declared
// definitions, never narrowed into the `u8` member because it is the only numeric one.  The lone
// definition `one(5)` beside it compiles (the sibling cell).  One refusal per file.
//
// ⚠ The rule also asks the refusal to name "the typed spelling that cures it"; this diagnostic
//   names the definitions only (the lambda refusal names its cure).  The cell matches what is
//   said; the missing cure is reported to the walk as a diagnostic deviation.
//
// @falsified-at: none — a conformance guard; the refusal fired as expected on 18594597b,
//   interpret and native.
// CHANNEL: the compile (the expected diagnostic).
// ARMED: no instrument.
// WITNESS: the diagnostic names the synthesised argument type and the declared members.
// HOLDS: a `u8`-typed local (`b: u8 = 5; hn(b)`) reaches `hn(u8)` EXACT.
// @EXPECT_ERROR: no definition of `hn` takes (integer) — declared: hn(u8), hn(text); a literal takes no width from the call — write `5 as u8`
//
// ── an-untyped-lambda-to-a-free-set-is-refused-naming-the-cure ──
//
// AN UNTYPED LAMBDA TO A FREE SET IS REFUSED, NAMING THE MEMBERS AND THE CURE
// (`@FR-Disp-Hint`): a name with several definitions offers no parse hint, so `|x| { x }` has
// nothing to type `x` from; the call is refused at the lambda, naming the definitions and the
// typed spelling `fn(x: <type>) -> <ret> { … }` that cures it — never typed from the first
// member.  The attic holds the `self`-set shape (`1926`); this is the FREE set.  One refusal per
// file.  The body does not read `x`: a body that does draws a second, cascading error — the
// sibling `a-refused-lambda-that-reads-its-parameter-says-only-the-refusal.loft`.
//
// @falsified-at: none — a conformance guard; the refusal fired as expected on 18594597b,
//   interpret and native.
// CHANNEL: the compile (the expected diagnostic).
// ARMED: no instrument.
// WITNESS: the diagnostic names both members and the typed spelling.
// HOLDS: a typed lambda picks its member (`1926b` in the attic).
// @EXPECT_ERROR: cannot infer the type of lambda parameter 'x': `hl` is hl(fn(integer) -> integer) and hl(fn(text) -> text), so no single definition types the lambda — write its types, `fn(x: <type>) -> <ret> { … }`

fn hn(x: u8) -> text { "u8 {x}" }
fn hn(t: text) -> text { "text {t}" }

fn test_a_literal_needing_a_width_at_a_set_is_refused() {
  println(hn(5));
}

fn hl(f: fn(integer) -> integer) -> text { "int {f(2)}" }
fn hl(f: fn(text) -> text) -> text { "text {f("a")}" }

fn test_an_untyped_lambda_to_a_free_set_is_refused_naming_the_cure() {
  println(hl(|x| { 7 }));
}
